{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space is big, and it’s getting bigger. But\nwhere does all that new space actually come"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from? And is it popping into existence all\naround you right now? Is that why the remote"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11360,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "control is always further away than I thought?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nearly a hundred years ago, a collection of\nobservations and theoretical ideas came together"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to reveal the universe is expanding on the\nlargest scales. The distant galaxies are all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "racing away from us, and interpreted through\nthe lens of Einstein’s general theory of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35920,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity, this only makes sense if all distances\neverywhere are increasing evenly. This revelation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42960,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "led us to the discovering of the beginning\nof the universe at the big bang, and lets"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47440,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us predict its future - it’ll probably expand\nforever, FYI."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52240,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know the effect of cosmic expansion on\nthe most enormous scales, but what about the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59440,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here and the now? If space \nis expanding everywhere, "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63440,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is it expanding inside the solar system, inside"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66640,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you? And what does it even mean for space\nto be expanding? Does that stretching stretch"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73360,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space thin? Will it eventually snap like overdrawn\ntaffy? Or does new space get created on the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80480,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fly - in which case, where does it come from?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Objects in space tend to move around due to\nnearby gravitational influences - planets"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit stars, stars orbit in the mutual gravity\nof their galaxies, galaxies whirl and collide"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in local groups and clusters. But if you expand\nyour view to large enough scales, that local"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "motion or “peculiar velocity” is small\ncompared to the fact that distant points are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106640,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving apart - receding - and the further\ndistant the faster the recession. We call"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113040,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this the Hubble flow, after Edwin Hubble,\nthe guy who first measured the rate of expansion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119200,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Hubble flow could be interpreted as everything\nmoving THROUGH space, but there’s a cleaner"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125360,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "picture. Soon after Einstein finished general\nrelativity, four scientists independently"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131440,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solved its equation for the entire universe,\ngiving us the Friedman-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138080,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or FLRW metric. You can think of a metric\nas the coordinate system of a patch of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144960,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Similar to how longitude and latitude and\nthe coordinate system of the 2-D surface of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149200,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Earth, a metric in GR is the coordinate\nsystem of a chunk of spacetime with 3 spatial"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156800,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and 1 temporal dimensions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158960,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And just as latitude and longitude describe\nthe spherical geometry of the surface of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163600,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth, the FLRW metric gives the geometry\nor shape of the universe. The spatial part"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171200,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of that geometry could be the 3-D analog of\nour surface of a sphere, which would be a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space that loops back on itself in all directions.\nOr it could be an infinite plane, either flat"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181920,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or hyperbolic. These shapes can be thought\nof as 2-D slices of 3-D spaces. But those"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3-D spaces are also just slices out of 4-D\nspacetimes - representing single instants"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194800,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in time. If you use general relativity to\ntrack how the FLRW metric changes over time,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201200,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you find that it has to change - either growing\nor shrinking in size everywhere evenly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207520,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The FLRW metric makes some pretty big assumptions\n- that the matter in the universe is perfectly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evenly spread out - homogeneous, and it looks\nthe same in all directions - isotropic. That"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219760,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seems to be a good representation of the universe\non its largest scales. But keep this assumption"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224880,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in mind. It’ll be important to notice when\nit’s wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, just for laughs let’s look at the math.\nThis is the FLRW metric - it’s basically"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234160,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pythagorus for 4-D spacetime - the squared\nproper distance between two points is the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sum of the squares of x, y \u0026 z, but here adding\nthe dimension of time. And this thing is the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246960,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scale factor, which represents the overall\nsize of the universe, and this is the thing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251360,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that has to change. In our universe it’s\ngetting bigger. When we say “the universe”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or “space” is expanding, we mean the scale\nfactor is getting bigger. But what does that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262560,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mean beyond the math? What’s physically\nhappening?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266080,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a classic classroom representation\nof the expanding universe that’s actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270480,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pretty useful here. Glue galaxies to a balloon\nand inflate. The galaxies recede from each"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276480,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other with apparent speeds that are proportional\nto distance. But the balloon analogy isn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even really an analogy - at any one instant\nit’s a fair representation of a 2-D slice"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288160,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out of a closed universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290080,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and its growth represents\nthe increase in the scale factor over time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294640,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The FLRW metric - the coordinate grid of this\nslice could be represented in spherical polar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301600,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coordinates. In that case it looks a lot like\nlatitude and longitude. Those lines are smooth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because of our founding assumptions of homogeneity\nand isotropy. But if we zoom in we can see"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the shape of the gridlines change. They\npull towards massive objects like galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319840,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact the shape of spacetime around massive\nobjects is NOT the FLRW metric because the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter isn’t spread out evenly. For example,\nnear a compact massive object the correct"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "description of the spacetime is the Schwarzschild\nmetric. The space in the Schwarzschild metric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338800,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by itself doesn’t expand - in fact space\nis pulled inwards - pinched - and it’s completely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345440,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "static over time. But what about if it's embedded\nin a larger FLRW spacetime? Does the underlying"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expanding sphere fight against the inward\npull of gravity? Is the space inside, say,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359120,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a galaxy growing but overcome by the gravitational\nattraction between the stars?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364560,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is no. Space within any gravitationally\nbound system is unaffected by the surrounding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansion. In the balloon analogy it’s tempting\nto think of the galaxy as being held together"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "despite the expanding material that it's attached\nto. But that’s not what’s happening. The"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382400,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational field isn’t somethin,g that\nlies on top of the fabric of spacetime. The"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386880,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational field IS the fabric of spacetime.\nIf the gravitational field of the galaxy is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this static, inwardly-pinched grid of the\nSchwarzschild metric, then that’s it. That’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the spacetime in that area. There isn’t\nanother underlying expanding grid that this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field has to fight against. In fact, the spacetime\ninside the Milky Way doesn’t even know that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409520,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe is expanding."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other way the balloon analogy fails is\nthat the fabric of spacetime doesn’t get"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415920,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stretched in the way the rubber gets stretched\n- it doesn’t thin out and it doesn’t build"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up tension in a way that can pull against\nthese embedded static fields. It’s as though"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the balloon adds more rubber as it inflates,\nalways maintaining the same level of stretch."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let's try something else. Lose another dimension\nof space so the universe becomes an expanding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437360,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ring instead of a ,sphere, with grid points\ninstead of gridlines. But then we can add"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443120,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the dimension of time into our picture so\nthat the expanding ring traces a sort of cone,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the points trace lines. This is nice because\nyou can show the changing rate of expansion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- perhaps a period of rapid accelerating growth\nduring inflation, then the expansion slows"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460720,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "under the influence of gravity, then dark\nenergy takes over and expansion accelerates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465520,
    "dDurationMs": 400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465920,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The expansion of space is seen in the divergence\nof our FLRW coordinate gridlines. Now the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distance between those lines represents the\ngrowing scale factor. Let’s zoom in again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What does an individual galaxy look like?\nIf the galaxy causes gridlines to be pulled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483360,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "together on a sphere, then on the ring it\ncauses points to be pulled together, and they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maintain that separation as the ring expands.\nSo on our expanding cone, we see distant gridlines"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "diverging, but nearby lines in a gravitational\nfield remain parallel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there is no constant tug of war between\nthe expanding universe and the gravitationally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507200,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bound systems it contains. That tug of war\ndid happen, but it happened a long time ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514160,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In certain regions expansion won, and threw\napart objects and the spacetime grids they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519680,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trace. But in sufficiently dense regions gravity\nwon and there the only evidence of the greater"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527120,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansion comes from the receding view of\ndistant galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531440,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I told you that space doesn’t get stretched\nout like rubber. In a sense new space gets"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "created as it expands. But what does that\nactually mean? Well this is a tough one - in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541040,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fact an impossible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542080,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one because we don’t have an accepted theory\nfor what that the fabric of space is actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546880,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made out of, if anything. That’s the province\nof a long-sought theory of quantum gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553760,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But let’s see what we can say."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general relativity, space can be infinitely\ndivided. That means we can start with a universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561760,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s small and grid it up and watch it\nexpand. The grid lines diverge, but we can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "choose to re-grid it at any point. What was\nonce a millimeter might become a meter and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573760,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the meter becomes a kilometer, but we can\njust redefine everything and start again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578800,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll never run out of subdivisions. Similarly,\nwe can take the universe of the present and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584960,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "define a grid of space - or a grid of points\non our 1-D ring universe. Then we rewind the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe and every single one of those points\ntraces a path back to the big bang. We can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596320,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make the starting grid as fine as we like\nand get the same result. Every point in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "modern universe can be traced back to a point\nat the big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606560,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is related to the idea \nof geodesic completeness, "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610320,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which we’ve talked about before. All spacetime"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613200,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "paths can be traced to the infinite future\nor past until they hit a singula,rity - the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620320,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big bang or a black hole. As the universe\nexpands, we don’t have new patches of space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appearing between the old ones to fill out\nthe universe. Geodesics don’t just pop out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632400,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of nowhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633440,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This all works if space is infinitely divisible.\nBut we know that at the smallest scales, general"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641120,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity comes into conflict with quantum\nmechanics. There is a smallest measurable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "length called the Planck length. When space\nexpands, what happens to this smallest length?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Does it expand too? Well the Planck length\nstays the same - it’s just defined as a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658000,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "combination of the Gravitational constant,\nthe Planck constant, and the speed of light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- so if those aren’t changing - and there’s\nno evidence that they change -then the Planck"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "length remains the same. But as the universe\nexpands it adds more and more of these “Planck"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672480,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lengths” which must expand out of the old\nPlanck lengths. That implies an infinitely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678000,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "divisible space emerging from within each\nPlanck length."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682320,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This apparent conundrum is related to something\ncalled the transplanckian problem, which is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to do with how Hawking radiation seems to\nemerge from sub-Planck-wavelength radiation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694800,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the event horizon of a black hole. Its\nsolution takes us to the realm of the holographic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700000,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "principle, and so we can’t go there today.\nBut long story short - even with a quantized"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fabric of space, it’s still possible to\nexpand it or condense it infinitely without"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713040,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "changing its fundamental nature. Empty space\ncan be rescaled infinitely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719600,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s even true if empty space contains\nsomething. Like dark energy. Empty space has"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726880,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a very weak energy density, even in the absence\nof particles. As space expands, that density"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doesn't change - remember, the balloon skin\ndoesn’t thin out. The result of this is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738080,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the total dark energy content of the\nuniverse depends on the amount of space in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742960,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe, which means dark energy increases\nas the universe expands. But again, this will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749200,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "never have any effect inside bound gravitational\nsystems. Its effect only manifests when there’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756000,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an enormous amount of empty space compared\nto the amount of matter. But the ratio of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760640,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "empty space to matter inside galaxies doesn’t\nchange. Now if the level of dark energy per"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767600,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unit volume were to increase, that would be\na different matter - and we talked about the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772400,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "resulting cataclysm in our episode on the\nbig rip."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776000,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there’s your answer. The infinite scalability\nof space means the universe can and probably"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783200,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will expand forever with no effect on this\nlittle bubble of relatively static space that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789840,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we call the Milky Way. \nWell, except for permanently "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793040,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "isolating our bubble from all of the others."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s just be grateful that we came into\nbeing soon enough to catch a glimpse of those"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "receding galaxies - they will spend most of\ncosmic time far beyond the horizon, in which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808960,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "case we never would have even known that we\nlive in an infinitely expanding spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815760,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re looking at your comments for\nthe last two episodes - there was the one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819920,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on John Archibald Wheeler’s Participatory\nUniverse, and the episode on the Higgs mechanism"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824720,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the mass of the W boson. Let’s do the\nlatter first."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830720,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "247tubefar asks How the heck do you weigh\na subatomic particle? Good question. Riley"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836960,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Schroeder quips “carefully and hypothetically”\n- which is correct in the sense that you don’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measure it not directly, like, not with a\nsubatomic set of scales. Fernando, who was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847280,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of the three of us who pulled our hair\nout writing this episode, gave the correct"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "answer: when a charged particle is created\nin a particle collider it travels through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857120,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the powerful magnetic fields of the detector.\nThe amount by which its path is deflected"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by that field depends on its mass. In the\ncase of the W boson, the particle actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866640,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decays too quickly to measure its mass “directly”,\nbut you can measure the masses and kinetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871040,
    "dDurationMs": 3907,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energies of its decay products and determine\nthe mass of the W."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874947,
    "dDurationMs": 1693,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some of you reasonably point out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that it is potentially misleading to use terms\nlike “observation” or “conscious awareness”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881920,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as the causal event that \ncollapses the wavefunction, "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884880,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or in this case manifests the universe. Rather"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than for example saying that “interaction”\nis doing the work. It’s very true that the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893360,
    "dDurationMs": 320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "choice"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893680,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of phrasing has led to all \nsorts of misunderstanding "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896800,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and quantum charlatanry. But I think the reason"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people like Wheeler and Bohr used these terms\nis because their conscious observation of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the system is literally the only event that\nthey can be sure actually happened. They were"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911200,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "being philosophically honest by adhering to\npure, stripped down logical inference in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916800,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "style of Descartes. They felt it dishonest\nto start their train of reasoning with statements"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about external factors that they couldn’t\ndirectly observe. Unfortunately, practitioners"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926560,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of quantum woo are not so careful and definitely\nnot so honest."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931200,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lyman Brown-Whitehill reminds us that the\nparticipatory isn’t the first of Wheeler’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935360,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wacky-sounding ideas. The other famous one\nis the one-electron universe, in which all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940160,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electrons are actually the same electron bouncing\nback and forward in time. Wacky as they are,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945680,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they tend to have legs these ideas. In his\nNobel prize acceptance speech, Richard Feynman"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952480,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "admitted to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953200,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stealing the time-traveling electron thing\nfrom his advisor, and using it to help develop"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958480,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum electrodynamics. So what can we build\nusing ideas from the participatory universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Meatsauce Z asks us to address the elephant\nin the room. If the participatory universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970720,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interpretation is true,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972320,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then what are entities that initiated the\nfirst interaction that brought everything"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into being? Actually, I think this is exactly\nthe issue the Wheeler was trying find his"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982000,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way around - the issue of infinite regress.\nHow to you identify a founding cause by tracing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the causal chain backwards? In Wheeler’s\nmind, the only universe that can exist is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993840,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the one that causes itself. That’s what\nhe meant by a “self-excited circuit”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 998640,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It put me in mind of the way the electron\nin the admittedly outdated Bohr model of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003440,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atom in a sense “creates itself” by constructive\ninterference - only electrons whose wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "peaks and valleys line up on each orbit can\nexist. Maybe the universal wavefunction is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016640,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the just that wavefunction in the space of "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019200,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinite possible wavefunctions \nthat constructively"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021840,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interferes to reinforce its own existence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025840,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Speaking of which, several of you notice that\nthis video was posted on 4/20. I swear that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was a coincidence. Like whoah dude."
    } ]
  } ]
}
